trace.content:
[THOUGHT]  
This is a classic presentation of duodenal atresia in a term newborn: onset of bilious vomiting and feeding intolerance within the first hours of life, absence of distension (suggesting proximal obstruction), and the pathognomonic “double bubble” sign on abdominal X-ray — representing air-filled stomach and proximal duodenum with no distal gas. The timing (6 hours old) effectively excludes other common causes of neonatal vomiting:  
- Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (presents at 2–8 weeks, non-bilious vomiting, palpable olive);  
- Malrotation with midgut volvulus (can present similarly but often with bilious vomiting *and* signs of ischemia/distress; “double bubble” is less specific here, and urgent contrast study or surgical exploration is needed);  
- Esophageal atresia (typically presents with choking/cyanosis/foaming at first feed, no gastric air on X-ray — i.e., “absent stomach bubble”);  
- Meconium ileus (associated with cystic fibrosis, presents later (24–48 hrs), with abdominal distension, failure to pass meconium, and “ground-glass” or “soap-bubble” appearance on X-ray).  
The absence of distension and presence of only two air bubbles strongly favors isolated duodenal obstruction — most commonly duodenal atresia. While annular pancreas or duodenal web can mimic this radiographically, duodenal atresia is the most common cause of the double bubble in the neonatal period and is strongly associated with trisomy 21 (~30–50% of cases), warranting prompt genetic evaluation.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Duodenal atresia

